    "golden_qa_pairs = [\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What is the main focus of this paper?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper explores microLED technology, particularly its integration with CMOS \"\n",
    "        \"microelectronics, and looks beyond traditional display applications toward uses \"\n",
    "        \"like nanosensors, optical neuromorphic networks, and chip-based microscopy.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What substrate do light-emitting GaN devices typically use, and why?\",\n",
    "        \"Light-emitting GaN devices are almost always grown on sapphire substrates rather \"\n",
    "        \"than silicon. Sapphire is more expensive and less thermally conductive than \"\n",
    "        \"silicon, but it allows fewer crystal defects, higher crystal quality, and is \"\n",
    "        \"transparent in the visible range, giving more flexibility in optical device design.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"Why is silicon preferred for electronic GaN devices instead of sapphire?\",\n",
    "        \"Electronic GaN devices are preferably grown on silicon wafers, which fits standard \"\n",
    "        \"CMOS design and foundry infrastructure, unlike light-emitting GaN devices which \"\n",
    "        \"favor sapphire for optical quality reasons.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What circuit design is commonly used to drive individual microLED pixels?\",\n",
    "        \"A 2T1C circuit — two transistors and one capacitor — is a common design for \"\n",
    "        \"driving microLEDs, used for both pulse amplitude modulation (PAM) and pulse \"\n",
    "        \"width modulation (PWM).\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What are the three key requirements for mass transfer technology in microLED \"\n",
    "        \"display manufacturing?\",\n",
    "        \"High placement accuracy (under 1 micron), a high transfer rate (over 100 million \"\n",
    "        \"per hour), and a high yield of functional pixels (over 99.999%).\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What material are elastomeric transfer stamps typically made from, and how do \"\n",
    "        \"they pick up microLEDs?\",\n",
    "        \"Elastomeric stamps are typically made from PDMS (poly(dimethylsiloxane)). They \"\n",
    "        \"pick up microLEDs via non-specific van der Waals forces when pressed against the \"\n",
    "        \"donor substrate, and release them onto the receiver substrate by reducing that \"\n",
    "        \"adhesion, for example by controlling peel velocity and temperature.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What is laser lift-off (LLO) used for in microLED fabrication?\",\n",
    "        \"Laser lift-off is used to remove the sapphire substrate from the MOVPE-LED stack, \"\n",
    "        \"which helps improve light extraction, optimize heat transfer, and enables \"\n",
    "        \"transferring LEDs onto flexible or foreign substrates.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What recent industry decision does the paper cite as evidence that microLED mass \"\n",
    "        \"production is still challenging?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper cites Apple's decision to postpone its microLED smartwatch project as \"\n",
    "        \"evidence that reliable, cost-effective hybrid integration of microLEDs is not yet \"\n",
    "        \"fully solved, though the paper frames this as a temporary delay rather than a \"\n",
    "        \"long-term setback.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"How does the minimum feasible microLED pixel size vary by display application?\",\n",
    "        \"Compact AR displays need the smallest pixels, roughly 1-10 micrometers. Smartwatch \"\n",
    "        \"and phone displays use medium pixel sizes around 10-50 micrometers, while large \"\n",
    "        \"screen TVs typically use larger pixels in the 50-100 micrometer range.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What is structured illumination microscopy using microLEDs, and how does it \"\n",
    "        \"differ from conventional microscopy?\",\n",
    "        \"Instead of illuminating a specimen all at once like a conventional microscope, \"\n",
    "        \"structured illumination microscopy switches individual nanoLEDs in an array on \"\n",
    "        \"and off one at a time. Since the illumination position is known precisely at \"\n",
    "        \"every moment, no imaging lens or focusing is required, and resolution is set by \"\n",
    "        \"the pitch of the nanoLEDs rather than conventional optics.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What resolution have nanoLEDs achieved in structured illumination experiments, \"\n",
    "        \"and what is the limiting distance requirement?\",\n",
    "        \"NanoLEDs with diameters as small as 200 nanometers have been demonstrated. However, \"\n",
    "        \"to translate that into actual spatial resolution, the sample needs to be within \"\n",
    "        \"roughly 400 nanometers of the nanoLED array's near field, which is difficult to \"\n",
    "        \"achieve given the layer structure required in nitride LEDs.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What is optical downscaling, and what power density can it achieve?\",\n",
    "        \"Optical downscaling is a technique where a microLED array's emission is optically \"\n",
    "        \"miniaturized (e.g. by a factor of 100) to shrink the effective pixel pitch below \"\n",
    "        \"the diffraction limit. This approach can achieve power densities as high as one \"\n",
    "        \"million watts per square centimeter.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"How are GaN microLEDs used in optogenetics research?\",\n",
    "        \"GaN-based microLEDs can excite channelrhodopsin (ChR2) molecules at a threshold \"\n",
    "        \"intensity using a 450 nanometer wavelength, triggering electrical excitation \"\n",
    "        \"responses in neural networks. This has been applied to study contraction behavior \"\n",
    "        \"in bioartificial cardiac tissue, with pixel widths between roughly 12.5 and 62.5 \"\n",
    "        \"micrometers used in these experiments.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What are the different operating regimes of an LED relevant to sensing \"\n",
    "        \"applications?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper describes multiple regimes: forward bias beyond the emission threshold \"\n",
    "        \"(the LED as a light source for optical sensing), the sub-threshold regime (where \"\n",
    "        \"the LED behaves as a non-emitting diode useful for mapping properties like \"\n",
    "        \"temperature or strain), and reverse operation where the LED's p-n junction can \"\n",
    "        \"act as a photodetector or energy harvester under external illumination.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What is visible light communication (VLC), and why are GaN LEDs suited to it?\",\n",
    "        \"Visible light communication uses LEDs both for illumination and simultaneous data \"\n",
    "        \"transmission to photodiode receivers. GaN LEDs are considered good candidates for \"\n",
    "        \"VLC transmitters due to their high average efficiency, and the paper describes \"\n",
    "        \"optimization approaches like rate-splitting multiple access combined with \"\n",
    "        \"reinforcement learning methods to maximize data rates.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What future light source is proposed as a successor to incoherent microLEDs, \"\n",
    "        \"and what challenge does it face?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper proposes moving from microLEDs to coherent microLaser sources based on \"\n",
    "        \"vertically emitting laser diodes (VCSELs), which require a vertical optical cavity \"\n",
    "        \"built from distributed Bragg reflectors (DBRs). A key challenge is that \"\n",
    "        \"conventional dielectric DBRs are non-conductive, complicating current flow through \"\n",
    "        \"the device structure.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"How might porous GaN help solve the DBR conductivity challenge for VCSELs?\",\n",
    "        \"Porous GaN can be fabricated using a dopant-dependent electrochemical etching \"\n",
    "        \"process, and because the etched channels are filled with air, it acts as a \"\n",
    "        \"low-refractive-index material suitable for DBRs, achieving peak reflectivity above \"\n",
    "        \"99% while remaining conductive.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What potential application does the paper describe as possibly higher-impact than \"\n",
    "        \"displays?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper highlights optical neuromorphic computing as a potentially even \"\n",
    "        \"higher-impact application than displays, citing the natural parallelism of optical \"\n",
    "        \"systems — where a single microLED can communicate with millions of detectors \"\n",
    "        \"simultaneously, similar to biological neural interconnects.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What compute efficiency does the paper claim is achievable with GaN-based optical \"\n",
    "        \"neuromorphic processing, compared to current AI hardware?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper states that such optical systems could achieve compute power exceeding \"\n",
    "        \"100 TOPS per watt, roughly two orders of magnitude better than the efficiency of \"\n",
    "        \"an NVIDIA H100 processor, which is commonly used today for large language models \"\n",
    "        \"and AI workloads.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    (\n",
    "        \"What does the paper identify as a key limiting factor for GaN technology \"\n",
    "        \"development going forward?\",\n",
    "        \"The paper points out that GaN foundries are not widely available, unlike silicon \"\n",
    "        \"electronics where foundry access is well established even for small-scale \"\n",
    "        \"research. It suggests that building out a GaN foundry ecosystem would \"\n",
    "        \"significantly help advance the technology.\"\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "]"
   ]
  },
