       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  ├── <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">  Input:            </span>  What is structured illumination microscopy using microLEDs, and how does it          <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  differ from conventional microscopy?                                                 <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">  Actual Output:    </span>  Structured illumination microscopy using microLEDs can be used for a type of         <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  microscopy known as chip microscopy. In this approach, the object is directly        <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  attached to the light-emitting array, and illuminated stepwise by switching on       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  and off each single nanoLED of an array.                                             <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>                                                                                       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  This method differs from conventional microscopes in that:                           <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>                                                                                       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  1. The object is not illuminated all at once.                                        <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  2. The illumination position is known at any point in time.                          <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  3. Focusing is not necessary since there are no optical parts, like lenses.          <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  4. Spatial resolution is not required from the detector (top), as the concept        <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  relies on knowing the illumination position.                                         <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>                                                                                       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  This approach can deliver images with resolutions given by the pitch of the          <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  nanoLEDs, which can be below the conventional diffraction limit of about 400 nm.     <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">  Expected Output:  </span>  Instead of illuminating a specimen all at once like a conventional microscope,       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  structured illumination microscopy switches individual nanoLEDs in an array on       <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  and off one at a time. Since the illumination position is known precisely at         <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  every moment, no imaging lens or focusing is required, and resolution is set by      <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  │   <span style=\"color: #008080; text-decoration-color: #008080; font-weight: bold\">                    </span>  the pitch of the nanoLEDs rather than conventional optics.                           <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
       "<span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>  └── <span style=\"font-style: italic\">Metrics                                                                                                  </span>  <span style=\"color: #800000; text-decoration-color: #800000\">│</span>\n",
